<%- include('partials/header', { title: 'Privacy Policy' }) %>

<div class="terms-container">
  <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
  <p class="terms-updated">Effective date: August 29, 2026</p>

  <p>This Privacy Policy explains what information AwardHome ("we", "us") collects, how we use it, and the
  choices you have. It applies to the AwardHome website and services (the "Service"). Our
  <a href="/terms">Terms of Service</a> describe the rules for using the Service.</p>

  <section>
    <h2>1. The Public Archive</h2>
    <p>The heart of AwardHome is an archive of competition results that dance competitions published — award
    names, placements, routine titles, studio names, and dancer names exactly as the competition announced
    them. This information was made public by the competitions themselves; we collect, organize, and preserve
    it. Because competitive dancers are often minors, many names in the archive belong to children —
    <strong>as they appeared in the competitions' own published results</strong>. Beyond that public record,
    the Service adds no personal details about a dancer unless the claimed profile's owner chooses to add
    them, and it offers controls to reduce a profile's visibility (Section 7).</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>2. Information You Provide</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Accounts:</strong> email address and a password (stored as a cryptographic hash, never in
        plain text), plus your role (family, studio, organizer).</li>
      <li><strong>Claims:</strong> when claiming a dancer profile we ask for the dancer's name and birthday,
        used to verify and distinguish dancers who share a name. Birthdays are never displayed publicly.</li>
      <li><strong>Profile content:</strong> photos, thank-you notes, bios, social handles, logos, and
        taglines you choose to add. These are reviewed before public display.</li>
      <li><strong>Studio and organizer material:</strong> rosters, results files, branding assets, and
        upcoming-event listings you submit for your organization.</li>
      <li><strong>Messages:</strong> feedback you send us, partner inquiries, and emails to
        hello@awardhome.com.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>3. Information Collected Automatically</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Session cookies:</strong> a cookie keeps you signed in and protects forms against forgery.
        We do not use advertising cookies or third-party ad trackers.</li>
      <li><strong>Server logs and security data:</strong> IP addresses and request data are processed to
        operate the Service, prevent abuse, and enforce rate limits.</li>
      <li><strong>First-party usage statistics:</strong> we count page views and clicks on some features (for
        example, event registration buttons) in aggregate form to understand what is useful. These counters
        are our own; no analytics identity is sold or shared with data brokers.</li>
      <li><strong>Error monitoring:</strong> when something breaks, technical details of the failure may be
        sent to our error-monitoring provider so we can fix it.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>4. Location ("Near me")</h2>
    <p>The Upcoming Events directory offers a "Near me" sort. If you allow the browser's location prompt, your
    coordinates are used for that page view only — to sort events by distance — and are
    <strong>never stored</strong>. Declining the prompt simply leaves the State filter, which works without
    location.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>5. How We Use Information</h2>
    <p>We use the information above to operate and improve the Service: displaying and safeguarding the
    archive, verifying claims, routing studio confirmations, sending the emails you'd expect (claim
    decisions, verification links, notifications relevant to your account), producing aggregate usage
    statistics, and keeping the Service secure. We send invitation and outreach emails to studios and
    organizers using publicly available business contact information; every such email honors our
    unsubscribe list.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>6. How Information Is Shared</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Publicly, by design:</strong> the archive, profile pages, and any content approved for
        public display are visible to anyone. Award cards are made to be shared.</li>
      <li><strong>Within your studio community:</strong> studio directors see claims routed to them for
        confirmation and manage their own rosters; private roster tags are visible only in management
        tools.</li>
      <li><strong>Service providers:</strong> we use reputable providers for hosting and backups, email
        delivery, and error monitoring. They process data only to provide their service to us.</li>
      <li><strong>Legal:</strong> we may disclose information when required by law or to protect the Service
        and its users.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We do <strong>not</strong> sell personal information, and we do not share it with advertisers or data
    brokers.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>7. Children's Privacy and Family Controls</h2>
    <p>Accounts require the holder to be at least 13; children's profiles are claimed and managed by a parent
    or legal guardian. A child's page shows the competition-published record plus only what the managing
    guardian adds. Guardians (and studio directors, for managed content) control:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>hiding any individual award card from the public page;</li>
      <li>keeping a dancer off public leaderboards and top-dancer lists;</li>
      <li>removing a profile from public name search entirely (the page then opens only by direct link);</li>
      <li>removing photos and notes at any time.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Anyone can flag a photo or note for review using the flag control on a card. Parents and guardians can
    contact <strong>hello@awardhome.com</strong> about their child's information at any time, including to
    request removal of content beyond the published competition record.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>8. Your Choices</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Visibility controls</strong> — described in Section 7, available on Manage Profile.</li>
      <li><strong>Email</strong> — outreach and invitation emails include one-click unsubscribe; account
        emails (verification, claim decisions) are sent only when triggered by activity.</li>
      <li><strong>Access, correction, deletion</strong> — email <strong>hello@awardhome.com</strong> to
        access or correct information we hold about you, close your account, or request deletion of content
        you added. The competition-published archive is a historical record and is generally retained, but
        visibility controls can remove a profile from search and public surfaces.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>9. Data Retention and Security</h2>
    <p>Account information is kept while your account is active and for a reasonable period afterward for
    security and legal purposes. The results archive is retained as a historical record. Backups are kept on
    encrypted infrastructure and expire on a rolling schedule. We protect data with industry-standard
    measures — HTTPS, hashed passwords, access controls — while noting that no online service can guarantee
    absolute security.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>10. International Visitors</h2>
    <p>The Service is operated from the United States and intended for the U.S. competitive-dance community.
    If you use it from elsewhere, your information will be processed in the United States.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>11. Changes to This Policy</h2>
    <p>We may update this policy as the Service evolves. Material changes will be posted here with a new
    effective date, and we will take reasonable steps to notify account holders.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>12. Contact</h2>
    <p>Privacy questions and requests: <strong>hello@awardhome.com</strong>, or the Send Feedback button on
    any page.</p>
  </section>
</div>

